The first thing on most coffee bags is a place: _Ethiopia_, _Colombia_, _Sumatra_. It's easy to skim past, but where a coffee was grown tells you more about how it'll taste than almost anything else on the label. It's also a pretty good hint about the rest of the label.

This is part one of [Understanding Coffee Beans](/coffee/guides/understanding-coffee-beans/), my guide to the four things on a bag that matter most.

## The bean belt

Coffee grows in a band around the equator, roughly between the Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn, often called the "bean belt." It needs warm days, no frost and plenty of rain, and within that band, the place a coffee comes from does a lot of the work before anyone roasts it.

## What the place changes

- **Altitude** is the big one. Higher farms are cooler, so the cherries ripen more slowly and the seeds come out denser and more complex, usually with more acidity. Bags often list it in meters above sea level ("masl"). Each country grades it a little differently, but roughly 1,200 to 1,350 masl and up counts as high-grown.
- **Climate and soil** matter the way they do for wine grapes. Volcanic soil, steady rain and cool nights all show up in the cup.
- **Local habits** may matter most. Each country tends to grow certain [varieties](/coffee/guides/coffee-varieties/) and [process](/coffee/guides/coffee-processing-methods/) them in certain ways. Kenya washes nearly everything, Brazil dries a lot of its coffee as naturals, and Sumatra wet-hulls. So when you know the origin, you can usually guess the rest of the label too.

## Reading origin on a bag

The more detail a bag gives, the better. A bag that just says "Colombia" could be anything. One that says _Colombia, Huila_, names the farm or washing station and lists _1,800 masl_ tells you the roaster knows exactly where it came from.

That level of detail is usually a sign of a **single origin** (one country, region or farm) rather than a **blend**. Blends aren't bad, though. Lots of great espresso is a blend, built for a consistent, balanced cup that tastes the same all year. Single origins are more like a snapshot of one place and one harvest.

